{"version":"1","id":"danslimmon","handle":"danslimmon","name":"Dan Slimmon","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1/logos/d430cfbef35d46fbae7383ec6696ab32","description":null,"is_squad":false,"x":-4.1813,"y":-1.211,"cluster_id":3,"posts_collected":28,"newest_post_at":"2025-06-25T00:00:00.000Z","oldest_post_at":"2023-03-06T19:15:46.000Z","features":{"hype_score":0.017857,"listicle_ratio":0.035714,"question_ratio":0.071429,"avg_read_time":4.035714,"summary_length_avg":285.178571,"title_length_avg":41.642857,"title_length_var":99.229592,"tag_entropy":2.714826,"tag_diversity":0.785714,"top_tag_share":0.285714,"avg_upvotes":1.357143,"median_upvotes":0.0,"avg_comments":0.071429,"comment_to_upvote_ratio":0.076923,"zero_engagement_share":0.678571,"viral_share":0.0,"posts_per_week":0.392519,"non_article_ratio":0.0,"author_present_share":0.0,"recency_skew":0.037037},"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[-2.2941,4.8088],"cadence_depth":[-7.0882,-4.5735],"hype_variety":[4.25,-4.6471],"title_style":[7.5294,7.2794]},"top_tags":[["devops",9],["sre",9],["performance",3],["monitoring",3],["backend",3],["postgresql",3],["productivity",2],["leadership",2]],"sample_titles":{"representative":["Leading incidents when you\u2019re junior","The queueing shell game","Interviewing engineers for diagnostic skills"],"outlier":["Explaining the fire"]},"recent_posts":[{"id":"kvIEaH1Yc","title":"Lecture: Queueing theory on a cocktail napkin","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2025/06/25/lecture-queueing-theory-on-a-cocktail-napkin/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/lecture-queueing-theory-on-a-cocktail-napkin-kvieah1yc","summary":"A lecture presentation on applying queueing theory principles to software service reliability and performance. The speaker emphasizes how understanding basic queueing theory can revolutionize SRE practices and improve system health monitoring.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/6e72a65ec6cb08e5133229820249483b?_a=AQAEulh","num_upvotes":1,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2025-06-25T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"q5PynAA36","title":"did u ever read so hard u accidentally wrote?","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2025/03/14/did-u-ever-read-so-hard-u-accidentally-wrote/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/did-u-ever-read-so-hard-u-accidentally-wrote--q5pynaa36","summary":"A production Postgres database owner investigates periodic spikes in WALWrite processes and discovers the role of read-only queries in dirtying pages through hint bits. The spikes are traced back to a Datastream backfill and an ongoing transaction that periodically causes write contention despite the read-only nature of the workload.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/cc0241e28da76cf9a3e9ac422d931e46?_a=AQAEuj9","num_upvotes":2,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2025-03-14T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"0xxZGuMoj","title":"Are operations roles truly unnecessary in tech?","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2025/02/11/is-ops-a-bullshit-job/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/is-ops-a-bullshit-job--0xxzgumoj","summary":"David Graeber\u2019s book *Bullshit Jobs: A Theory* discusses jobs that employees find pointless and unnecessary. The post explores the idea of whether operational roles in tech, like System Administrator and DevOps Engineer, are considered bullshit jobs. It argues that while some tech roles may seem like 'duct taper' jobs created to solve non-existent problems, operations roles are crucial for bridging the gap between software development and real-world application, thus providing essential value.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/cff56d69235a4be7a18c8ddde2d2f01d?_a=AQAEuj9","num_upvotes":3,"num_comments":1,"created_at":"2025-02-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"sNpJt2k3N","title":"Incident SEV scales are a waste of time","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2025/01/29/incident-sev-scales-are-a-waste-of-time/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/incident-sev-scales-are-a-waste-of-time-snpjt2k3n","summary":"The post argues against the effectiveness of severity (SEV) scales in incident response, suggesting that complexity-based classification can offer better support. Using analogies like the Fujita scale for tornadoes, it points out that SEV scales often require time-consuming research and may not provide immediate actionable insights. Instead, the complexity-based Incident Command System (ICS) offers a more practical approach by focusing on the coordination and resources needed for different types of incidents.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/992596ab60739f83bdf06eaf17a0cdbb?_a=AQAEuj9","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2025-01-29T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"s5r7BSFsj","title":"The queueing shell game","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2024/08/21/the-queueing-shell-game/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/the-queueing-shell-game-s5r7bsfsj","summary":"Queues form when more requests hit a resource than can be handled concurrently. Simply expanding the queue size doesn't resolve the underlying issues, as queues will continue to form. Efforts to eliminate queues only shift their location within the system. Effective management of queues is crucial to avoid systemic failures, such as buffer overruns and exponential latency spikes.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/7584656b7408f7edc6095af9ecd1c152?_a=AQAEuiZ","num_upvotes":10,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2024-08-21T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"DFbWdONcK","title":"Podcast: Small Batches with Adam Hawkins","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2024/08/20/podcast-small-batches-with-adam-hawkins/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/podcast-small-batches-with-adam-hawkins-dfbwdonck","summary":"Recently, I had the pleasure of being interviewed by Adam Hawkins on his podcast, Small Batches. We discussed a wide range of topics during the episode.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/80f2a99a11b87f7dc3c7391d61b64c79?_a=AQAEuiZ","num_upvotes":2,"num_comments":1,"created_at":"2024-08-20T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"7brjyZtMK","title":"Putting a meaningful dent in your error backlog","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2024/08/15/putting-a-meaningful-dent-in-your-error-backlog/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/putting-a-meaningful-dent-in-your-error-backlog-7brjyztmk","summary":"Managing a bloated error tracking backlog can seem hopeless, but by excluding specific behaviors and addressing them, incremental value can be created. This approach makes systems more reliable and documentation valuable for knowledge transfer. Regularly scrutinizing and fixing errors can transform raw error data into meaningful insights and prevent errors from dominating the system.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--VDukGCjf--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2002","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2024-08-15T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"L4A4AIM9A","title":"No Observability Without Theory: The Talk","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2024/07/03/no-observability-without-theory-the-talk/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/no-observability-without-theory-the-talk-l4a4aim9a","summary":"The author shared their experience of presenting the talk \u201cNo Observability Without Theory\u201d at Monitorama 2024, which they highly recommend as the best tech conference. The talk, adapted from an older blog post, included some fun elements like making up medical jargon. The post includes the slides and a video of the presentation.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/dee83558c696bd7eee6a619c8f7b8810?_a=AQAEuiZ","num_upvotes":3,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2024-07-03T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"MemFZBDFx","title":"Leading incidents when you\u2019re junior","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2024/06/27/leading-incidents-when-youre-junior/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/leading-incidents-when-you-re-junior-memfzbdfx","summary":"Junior engineers may find themselves leading incident responses, even guiding more experienced colleagues. The role of an incident lead is primarily about maintaining coordination and common understanding among team members, rather than technical problem-solving. By asking fundamental questions and ensuring clarity, junior engineers can effectively manage incidents, helping the entire team stay aligned.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/25cf588b0f739ea410797a909559e5b6?_a=AQAEuiZ","num_upvotes":12,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2024-06-27T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"kEfhMyNLQ","title":"Fight knowledge decay with a rich Incident Summary","url":"https://blog.danslimmon.com/2024/06/14/fight-knowledge-decay-with-a-rich-incident-summary/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/fight-knowledge-decay-with-a-rich-incident-summary-kefhmynlq","summary":"Preserving and repairing joint understanding during incident response is crucial. The traditional timeline format for scribe notes has drawbacks, leading to longer onboarding and potential misinterpretations. Instead, focusing on creating an incident summary at the top of the document helps maintain joint understanding, provides a concise overview of the problem, and encourages better planning for shorter incidents.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/5734cceb418553f3b38637f4f68e3a1b?_a=AQAEuiZ","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2024-06-14T00:00:00.000Z"}],"similar_handles":["lastweekinaws","kotlinwkly","juliabl","appcoda","abinoda"],"opposite_handles":["tbpn","alexfinn","felixrieseberg","ryolu_","kloss_xyz"],"similar":[{"id":"lastweekinaws","handle":"lastweekinaws","name":"The Last Week in AWS","is_squad":false,"x":-3.8857,"y":-1.1053,"cluster_id":3,"posts_collected":41,"image":"https://media.daily.d